      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The main barriers reported by the patients with claudication are related to claudication symptoms. However, it remains unclear whether these barriers are associated with physical activity levels in these patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to analyze the barriers to and the factors associated with physical activity (PA) in intermittent claudication (IC) patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The sample included 150 IC Brazilian patients and mean age 64\u2009±\u20099 years old. Sociodemographic factors, comorbid conditions and cardiovascular risk factors, personal and environmental barriers to PA, and walking capacity (claudication onset distance-COD and peak walking distance-PWD) were obtained. PA was assessed using a pedometer over seven consecutive days."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Patients performed 6,041\u2009±\u20093,166 steps/day. The most prevalent personal and environmental barriers to PAs were exercise-induced pain and the presence of obstacles that aggravate the leg pain. Multiple linear regression showed that level of PA was inversely associated with age (β\u2009=\u2009-81.13; p\u2009<\u20090.001), lack of green areas (β\u2009=\u2009-1363.54; p\u2009<\u20090.001), and positively associated with PWD (β\u2009=\u20093.07; p\u2009<\u20090.001)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Older IC patients who live in neighborhoods that lack green areas to walk in, and who have poor walking capacity present lower levels of PA."
        }
      ]},
